  HARDCAP CHECK: the attacker sizes the FRONT-RUN to fit inside D, so the back-run
  retraces budget that was never credited.  Read 'sw esc'.

  FIX C - 'charge only when extension and retracement fall in the same block'.
  ALREADY THE DESIGN.  T0 and both budgets reset every block; the fee contains no
  cross-block state whatsoever.  There is nothing to change - see PART 2.

  FIX D - EMA / TWAP reference instead of the raw block-open tick.
  NOT SIMULATED, and argued dead rather than tested: an EMA is by construction MORE
  lagged than the pool's own current price, so it makes the reference staler, not
  fresher; and it introduces cross-block state, which reopens 2.4c poisoning at a
  horizon the extension cap no longer bounds.

======================================================================================================================
PART 8 - THE RESET IS THE EXEMPTION.  Found by following PART 2, not hypothesised.
  T0 is re-set to the pool's OWN price at every block open.  Therefore the first swap of
  a block can never be a retracement: there is nothing behind it to retrace.  Every
  top-of-block swap is fee-exempt BY CONSTRUCTION.  That is the same shape as Hardcap's
  first-swap exemption.  So: front-run + victim in block N (front-run is an extension,
  free), unwind at the TOP of block N+1 (extension from the new T0, free).
  Cost = 2 LP fees + losing the race for the first slot.  SWITCHBACK fee = 0.
======================================================================================================================
  vol 60% ann, LP fee 5bps         ( 980 attackable victims)  in-block:  27.9% still profitable, keeps   1.7% of gross  |  cross-block:  82.6% profitable, keeps  90.6%
  vol 60% ann, LP fee 30bps        (  23 attackable victims)  in-block:   0.0% still profitable, keeps   0.0% of gross  |  cross-block:  95.7% profitable, keeps  95.7%
  vol 120% ann, LP fee 5bps        ( 972 attackable victims)  in-block:  27.1% still profitable, keeps   1.7% of gross  |  cross-block:  83.3% profitable, keeps  89.7%
  vol 120% ann, LP fee 30bps       (  35 attackable victims)  in-block:   8.6% still profitable, keeps   0.3% of gross  |  cross-block:  94.3% profitable, keeps  92.5%
  CONTROL: total SWITCHBACK fee charged on every top-of-block unwind above = 0.00 (asserted).

  The attacker's only remaining cost is the race for the first slot of block N+1.
  If they lose it, the top-of-block arb takes the displacement instead.  Break-even
  win probability q* solves  q * (cross-block profit) = (in-block profit):
       vol   LP fee    q* (must WIN this often)
       60%       5b                      0.019
       60%      30b                      0.000
      120%       5b                      0.018
      120%      30b                      0.003
  q* is the fraction of first slots the attacker must win for the cross-block route to
  beat the in-block route.  A LOW q* means the exemption is cheap to reach.
  On a 200ms single-sequencer chain (Unichain, the target) the boundary is 200ms and
  the first slot is bought with priority fee - MODELLED, NOT MEASURED.

