  The XB columns above assume the attacker lands on a RESET BOUNDARY, which is what a
  rational attacker does: block.number % N is public, so the boundary is scheduled, not
  raced.  The attacker gives up nothing but frequency - roughly 1 opportunity in N.
    N   XB keep (at a boundary)   x 1/N frequency  in-block keep   better route
    1                     87.3%            87.27%           1.6%    CROSS-BLOCK
    2                     82.0%            41.02%           1.4%    CROSS-BLOCK
    5                     81.0%            16.20%           1.5%    CROSS-BLOCK
   10                     78.1%             7.81%           1.4%    CROSS-BLOCK
   30                     69.6%             2.32%           1.0%    CROSS-BLOCK
   60                     69.3%             1.15%           1.1%    CROSS-BLOCK

======================================================================================================================
PART 12 - CAN THE ATTACKER STEER THE REFERENCE?  The 2.4c dust-poison attack aimed at
  T0 instead of at the budget.  Under an EMA, biasing T0 by DELTA ticks requires holding
  the tick DELTA/alpha ticks away at the moment the reference is sampled.
======================================================================================================================
  alpha   push for +5t bias   LP fees on the push   + SWITCHBACK on the unwind       total
   1.00                5.0t                    5$                           0$          5$
   0.50               10.0t                   10$                           5$         15$
   0.25               20.0t                   20$                          30$         50$
   0.10               50.0t                   50$                         225$        275$
   0.02              250.0t                  248$                       6,159$      6,407$
  The steerer pushes at block close and unwinds at the top of the next block - i.e.
  they use the same exemption we are trying to close.  Under alpha<1 that unwind is
  itself charged, which is what makes steering expensive.  But note the cost scales
  with 1/alpha, so a SMALL alpha makes steering expensive - and a
  small alpha is exactly what PART 10b shows is unusable in a trend.  The two
  requirements are opposed: alpha must be small to resist steering and to close the
  cross-block route, and large to avoid taxing trend-following flow.
  NOT SIMULATED: the steerer only PROFITS if they also capture the resulting fee as an
  in-range LP (2.4d).  This rig does not model the fee's destination at all.

======================================================================================================================
PART 13 - WHAT DOES alpha<1 COST?  A carried reference taxes more of the corrective arb
  (shortfall 26% -> 48% at alpha=0.5), so price discovery is more impaired.  PART 6b's
  method: sandwiches OFF, so there is no extraction to prevent and no sandwich fee
  revenue to lose.  Any movement is the reference policy alone.
======================================================================================================================
policy                   ref lag   staleness  arb shrt  poolvsHODL   SWB rev  LP total   honest bill/blk
EMA alpha=1.00             0.00t      11.31t     27.2%       15.59     13.07     28.65              8.75
EMA alpha=0.75             3.67t      11.75t     41.3%       14.91     16.19     31.10              8.22
EMA alpha=0.50             7.05t      12.61t     48.5%       14.47     17.36     31.82              9.16
EMA alpha=0.25            10.91t      13.55t     54.5%       14.20     19.04     33.24             10.70
EMA alpha=0.10            14.29t      14.06t     57.5%       14.07     20.38     34.44             11.91
  Compare 'poolvsHODL' across rows: that is the LP cost of the impaired price
  correction, with everything else held fixed.

  Steering economics at alpha=0.5, from the measured outputs above rather than assumed:
  PART 10 gives d(honest bps)/d(ref lag) = (4.14-3.63)/(11.41-7.34) = 0.125 bps/tick.
  Honest notional/block = $24,731, so a 1-tick bias earns LPs $0.31/block.
  A 5-tick bias under alpha=0.5 decays as 5,2.5,1.25,... = 10 tick-blocks total
  => total extra fee $3.09, against a measured steering cost of
  $15 (PART 12).  The steerer also receives only their LP SHARE of that fee.
  => steering is about 5x underwater at alpha=0.5.
  REASONED FROM MEASURED OUTPUTS, NOT DIRECTLY SIMULATED: no steering agent was run,
  and the fee's destination (2.4d) is not modelled anywhere in this rig.

======================================================================================================================
PART 14 - THE OBVIOUS NEXT ATTACK: just WAIT.  Under an EMA the reference catches up
  geometrically, so an attacker who holds the position k blocks faces only (1-alpha)^k
  of the charge.  Does alpha=0.5 survive that?
  ATTACKER-FAVOURABLE BY CONSTRUCTION: this probe assumes NOBODY trades in the waiting
  blocks, so the displacement the attacker is sitting on is still there when they
======================================================================================================================
  unwind.  In reality the top-of-block corrective arb takes it at N+1.  Read these as
  an UPPER BOUND on the attacker.
  alpha       k=1       k=2       k=3       k=5      k=10   in-block
   1.00     90.7%     91.7%     92.4%     87.8%     91.4%       1.9%
   0.75      3.2%     25.7%     73.4%     88.7%     84.3%       1.3%
   0.50      1.5%      5.4%      9.8%     52.9%     91.2%       1.8%
   0.25      1.7%      1.4%      1.5%      3.2%     28.6%       1.7%
  Waiting DOES erode the fix - but the attacker cannot actually wait, because the
  top-of-block corrective arb at N+1 takes the displacement they are holding.  That is
  the 2.4a inventory-risk argument, and unlike at alpha=1 it is now TRUE: the wait is
  measured in EMA half-lives, not in one block boundary.

  BLOCK TIME IS NOT A FREE PARAMETER.  The EMA decays per BLOCK, so alpha must be set
  in WALL-CLOCK terms or Unichain's 200ms blocks shrink the protection 60x.
  For a fixed wall-clock half-life the steady-state lag is INVARIANT to block time:
    lag_sd ~ sigma_block / sqrt(2*alpha) ; sigma_block ~ 1/sqrt(B) ; alpha ~ 1/B
    => lag_sd ~ constant.  Every tick number in PART 10 therefore carries over.
    Ethereum 12s     alpha for a 12s half-life = 0.5000
    Unichain 200ms   alpha for a 12s half-life = 0.0115

======================================================================================================================
PART 15 - BLOCK TIME.  Everything above ran 12s blocks.  Unichain is 200ms.  The
  wall-clock-invariance argument in the REFERENCE-CARRY section is ANALYTIC; this is the
  test of it.
  RESCALING, stated because it is a judgement call and it drives the answer:
    sigma_block  -> annual/sqrt(seconds_per_year/spb)          [falls as 1/sqrt(B)]
    swaps/block  -> 3.0 * spb/12                               [flow is a WALL-CLOCK rate]
    drift/block  -> mu * spb/12                                [same reason]
    blocks run   -> 4000 * 12/spb                              [same wall-clock span]
    alpha        -> 1 - 0.5^(spb/12)                           [12s wall-clock half-life]
    TRADE SIZE   -> HELD CONSTANT PER SWAP.  This is the judgement call.  A $5,000 trade
      is a $5,000 trade whatever the block time, so RETAIL TICK IMPACT IS CONSTANT PER
      SWAP (5 ticks), NOT per block.  The alternative - holding impact constant per
      block - would make every Unichain swap 60x larger, which is not a market.
      If that choice is wrong, section 2's transfer is wrong; it is the one input here
      that is a modelling decision rather than a consequence.
======================================================================================================================

  sigma_block: 12s =  3.701 ticks , 200ms =  0.478 ticks  (ratio 7.75 ~ sqrt(60)=7.75)
  alpha for a 12s half-life: 12s -> 0.5000 , 200ms -> 0.011486

